Subject: The regexp required-literal and first-character scans of a 16-bit subject compare eight units at a time

What this does
  - new `js_regexp_find_u16` (SSE2 compare of eight 16-bit units, scalar tail and scalar where SSE2 is absent; `emmintrin.h` included under `__SSE2__` beside it)
  - `js_regexp_find_literal` on a 16-bit subject without case folding searches the literal's rarest unit with it and checks the whole literal around each hit; with case folding the scalar loop stays
  - `js_regexp_indexof_char_range` uses it for 16-bit subjects

Why we need it
  The 16-bit branch of `js_regexp_find_literal` was a scalar loop and was the hottest part of the function (5.5% of the regexp row at fixed work). Regexp row -4.5% cycles, -6.6% instructions.

Soundness
  Same candidates in the same order, each checked by the unchanged `js_regexp_lit_at`; the vector loop returns the first equal unit. 1260 cases of 16-bit subjects with required literals at every offset (lengths 0-69, three phases, six literals incl. non-Latin-1) equal an `indexOf` reference; the row checksum passes.

